        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the pot by forming the highest-ranking 5-card Poker hand using any of the seven cards dealt, utilizing the current wild cards.',
          gameplay: '1. Initial Deal: 2 down, 1 up. Betting.\n2. 4th, 5th, 6th Streets: One upcard each, with a betting round after each. If a Queen is dealt, watch the next card closely!\n3. The River: One final down-card. Final betting round.',
          keyRules: [
            "Natural is Better: In some house rules, a 'Natural' hand (without wild cards) beats a wild hand of the same rank.",
            "No Queen No Game: If no Queen appears face-up by the end of dealing, many play that there are NO wild cards, or that the hand is a 'misdeal' and everyone re-antes.",
            'Winning condition: Win the pot by forming the highest-ranking 5-card Poker hand using any of the seven cards dealt, utilizing the current wild cards.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Don't get attached to your hand until the 6th card is dealt. A pair of Aces is great, but if 9s suddenly become wild on the 6th street, you might be instantly losing to a Three-of-a-Kind.",
          intermediate: "The 'Queen Hunt': If you are looking for a specific rank to fill a Straight, hoping for a Queen to appear can be a viable strategy, as it effectively doubles your 'outs'.",
          advanced: "Psychology of Shifting Wilds: If the wild card changes late in the hand, players who were betting aggressively on the previous wild card will often go into 'check' mode. Use this hesitation to bluff if you can represent the new wild card.",
          tips: [
            'Always keep track of which Queens have already been dealt. If 3 Queens are on the table, the wild card is unlikely to change again.',
            "Five-of-a-Kind: Remember that with wild cards, a 'Five-of-a-Kind' is the highest possible hand, beating even a Royal Flush.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
